<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Oracles Distribution</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also analyze the distribution of oracle types used by <span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TransFuzz</span> to detect the bugs listed in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.1. Experimental Setup ‣ 4. Implementation and Evaluation ‣ LLM-Powered Silent Bug Fuzzing in Deep Learning Libraries via Versatile and Controlled Bug Transfer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.2. RQ1: Bug Discovery ‣ 4. Implementation and Evaluation ‣ LLM-Powered Silent Bug Fuzzing in Deep Learning Libraries via Versatile and Controlled Bug Transfer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, and the results are shown in Fig. <a href="#A1.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ Appendix A Oracles Distribution ‣ LLM-Powered Silent Bug Fuzzing in Deep Learning Libraries via Versatile and Controlled Bug Transfer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>.
Among them, <span id="A1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Crash Detection</span> accounts for the largest portion (46 cases), indicating that program crashes remain the easiest type of bug to trigger and are the most representative.
<span id="A1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Value Conformance</span> (12 cases) highlights the core challenge faced by DL libraries in ensuring numerical computation reliability.
<span id="A1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Error Message Analysis</span> (12 cases) and <span id="A1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Special Value Detection</span> (2 cases) capture non-crash bugs such as misleading warnings or unexpected special values.
<span id="A1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Device Consistency</span> (2 cases), <span id="A1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Eager/Compile Consistency</span> (1 case), and <span id="A1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Eager/JIT Consistency</span> (1 case) reflect the need to verify consistency across hardware and execution environments, not only in output values but also in performance behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As an example of <span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Device Consistency</span>, <span id="A1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TransFuzz</span> can detect performance degradation across devices.
Given a known bug where <span id="A1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">torch.linalg.svd</span> exhibits a significant GPU performance regression compared to CPU, <span id="A1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TransFuzz</span> transfers this bug pattern to <span id="A1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">torch.functional.pca_lowrank</span>, which implements a similar matrix factorization algorithm, and constructs an oracle that compares GPU and CPU execution time, reporting a bug when GPU execution is an order of magnitude slower.
These results highlight that <span id="A1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TransFuzz</span> can automatically design diverse oracles based on bug behavior to detect different types of bugs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F12" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.23065v1/oracleDistribution.svg" id="A1.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/286;" width="476" height="286"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F12.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 12</span>. </span><span id="A1.F12.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Distribution of oracle types used to detect the bugs listed in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.1. Experimental Setup ‣ 4. Implementation and Evaluation ‣ LLM-Powered Silent Bug Fuzzing in Deep Learning Libraries via Versatile and Controlled Bug Transfer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.2. RQ1: Bug Discovery ‣ 4. Implementation and Evaluation ‣ LLM-Powered Silent Bug Fuzzing in Deep Learning Libraries via Versatile and Controlled Bug Transfer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, showing the frequency of each oracle type.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Semantic Transfer Patterns for Silent Bug Discovery</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present an empirical analysis of semantic-level bug transfer enabled by <span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TransFuzz</span>. Table <a href="#A2.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Appendix B Semantic Transfer Patterns for Silent Bug Discovery ‣ LLM-Powered Silent Bug Fuzzing in Deep Learning Libraries via Versatile and Controlled Bug Transfer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> summarizes representative silent bugs discovered via <span id="A2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TransFuzz</span> through semantic-level transfer across APIs. In each case, the source and target APIs do not merely share naming or version-level similarity; instead, they exhibit a deeper semantic commonality, such as functional similarity, shared mathematical domains, type inference logic, or implementation-level dependencies.
Notably, most transfers are non-trivial: the involved APIs often reside in different modules or serve distinct usage scenarios, yet expose similar latent bugs due to shared semantics. This demonstrates that <span id="A2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TransFuzz</span> goes beyond testing “parallel APIs” and instead leverages LLM-guided reasoning to identify and exploit deep logical relationships between APIs, enabling effective bug transfer across diverse API boundaries.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T10" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T10.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 10</span>. </span><span id="A2.T10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Nine semantic transfer types identified by TransFuzz. Each type is defined by a shared semantic basis between the source and target APIs, enabling systematic bug transfer beyond API name similarity.</span></figcaption>
<div id="A2.T10.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:84.2pt;vertical-align:-40.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-75.7pt,18.5pt) scale(0.694905137890444,0.694905137890444) ;">
<table id="A2.T10.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A2.T10.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T10.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T10.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Transfer Type</span></td>
<td id="A2.T10.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T10.5.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Semantic Basis</span></td>
<td id="A2.T10.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T10.5.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Example API Pair</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T10.5.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T10.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="A2.T10.5.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Functional Similarity</span></td>
<td id="A2.T10.5.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="A2.T10.5.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Similar core operation (log-softmax)</span></td>
<td id="A2.T10.5.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="A2.T10.5.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">log_softmax <math id="A2.T10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> sparse.log_softmax</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T10.5.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T10.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="A2.T10.5.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Mathematical Domain</span></td>
<td id="A2.T10.5.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="A2.T10.5.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Similar mathematical domain (inverse hyperbolic)</span></td>
<td id="A2.T10.5.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="A2.T10.5.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">acosh <math id="A2.T10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> asinh_</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T10.5.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T10.5.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="A2.T10.5.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Type System Semantics</span></td>
<td id="A2.T10.5.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="A2.T10.5.1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Similar type inference logic (Python</span><math id="A2.T10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.T10.5.1.4.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Tensor)</span></td>
<td id="A2.T10.5.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="A2.T10.5.1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">tensor <math id="A2.T10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> default_collate</span></td></tr>
