</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>IR Definitions for Bug Types</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Sec. <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5. LLM-Powered Self-Validation ‣ 3. Design ‣ LLM-Powered Silent Bug Fuzzing in Deep Learning Libraries via Versatile and Controlled Bug Transfer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>, we use IR to rigorously define common bug types, thereby transforming the subjective problem of whether bugs are similar into a classification problem of bug types. Here, we provide the IR definitions for the remaining six common bug types:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.Ex3X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
